  {
    "episode_id": 92845,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92845.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "Professionally, it's probably the most consequential decision I made. Dylan, I've always said, then, even when I got elected, Dylan, in 2010, I would always say in every speech, I'm a conservative. I'm a libertarian. I'm a Tea Party conservative. First, I'm a Republican second because I am what I believe. I'm not the party I belong to. But I have been a Republican my whole life. And a year ago, almost exactly a year ago, I left that party. And it's scary because a lot of people like me, Dylan, a lot of them never Trumpers, went and joined the Democratic Party. I have all the respect in the world for Democrats. I really do. But I'm not a Democrat. Some of them want to stay and fight in the Republican Party. I think the Republican Party is hopeless. We've all had to make this decision. What do we do now? But it's really scary because, again, I'm older than you, Dylan. In my lifetime, in the rest of my life, I may never, ever fit in politically anywhere again. That's a scary thought.",
